This paper highlights the significant potential of graph neural networks (GNNs) in crafting intelligent representations of complex systems based on structured knowledge, with a focus on their application in tissue analysis. Leveraging GNNs, a tissue graph was created from the ALADIN database by integrating various anatomical, histological, genetic, and phenotypic attributes from humans and mice. Following extensive experimentation, a graph neural network model called RGCN was developed and utilized to generate compact, informative tissue embeddings. Subsequent analysis of these embeddings uncovered meaningful biological patterns, including phenotypic similarities between mice and humans, genetic influences on phenotypic variations, histological similarities correlated with genetic relatedness, and phenotypic similarities correlated with genetic relatedness. Tissue graph classification experiments also revealed that the embbedings produced by RGCN can accurately classify distinct phenotypic characteristics, such as tumor subtypes, disease syndromes, and anatomical locations. Furthermore, integration of multi-modal drug side effect effects into the graph and guided further exploration:
	1. side effect patterns enabled precise classification of tumor types;
	2. implicit drug-tumor associations uncovered by the model were validated experimentally and in literature, with re-ranking of drug indications leading to the potential for novel clinical applications.
